data.content:
## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — no equivalent exists
3. The idiomatic replacement is `CancellationToken` + `Task` with cooperative cancellation

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Async-native overload (preferred)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Not truly preemptive** | `Thread.Abort` killed work forcibly. `CancellationToken` is *cooperative* — the `work` delegate **must** check `token.IsCancellationRequested` or call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at safe points. Uncooperative work will run past the timeout. |
| **Uncooperative legacy code** | If you can't modify `work` to accept a token, wrap it in a `Task.Run` and **abandon** (not abort) the task on timeout. The thread leaks until it finishes naturally — this is by design in .NET 8. |
| **`default(T)` race condition** | The original code had a subtle bug: `result` could be read as `default` if the thread was aborted mid-assignment. The `Task`-based version eliminates this. |
| **Exception semantics** | The original wrapped in `AggregateException`. `Task.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` unwraps to the original exception, changing catch behavior for callers. Wrap explicitly if needed. |
| **Blocking with `GetResult()`** | The sync overload blocks the calling thread. Prefer the `async` overload to avoid threadpool starvation. |

### Handling Uncooperative Work (fire-and-forget fallback)

If you **cannot** modify the work delegate to accept a token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var task = Task.Run(work);

    if (task.Wait(timeout))
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();

    // Task is abandoned — thread will eventually complete on its own
    _ = task.ContinueWith(t => _ = t.Exception,
        TaskContinuationOptions.OnlyOnFaulted);

    throw new TimeoutException(
        $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
}
```

> ⚠️ This leaks a thread until `work` completes. Use only as a bridge during migration; refactor toward cooperative cancellation.
